# Tim Berners-Lee

Sir Timothy John Berners-Lee (born 1955) is an English computer scientist best known as the inventor of the World Wide Web. While working at the European Organization for Nuclear Research (CERN) in 1989, he proposed an information management system that linked hypertext documents over the internet, fundamentally transforming global communication, commerce, and information distribution. By late 1990, Berners-Lee had implemented the first successful communication between a Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) client and server, alongside the creation of the first web browser and web server. Crucially, he successfully advocated for the underlying code of the Web to be released into the public domain on a royalty-free basis, ensuring its rapid and universal adoption. Following his invention, he founded the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1994 to oversee the continued, collaborative development of web standards. He has also been a prominent advocate for data privacy, open government data, and the Semantic Web, later launching the Solid project to decentralize internet data ownership. In recognition of his foundational contributions to modern computing infrastructure, Berners-Lee was awarded the Turing Award in 2016 and was knighted by Queen Elizabeth II in 2004 for his services to the global development of the internet.

## Early Life and Education

Timothy John Berners-Lee was born on June 8, 1955, in London, England. His parents, Mary Lee Woods and Conway Berners-Lee, were both mathematicians and early computer scientists who worked on the Ferranti Mark 1, the first commercially available general-purpose electronic computer. This environment exposed him to computing and mathematics from an early age.

Berners-Lee attended Emanuel School in southwest London before enrolling at The Queen's College, Oxford, in 1973. He graduated in 1976 with a first-class Bachelor of Arts degree in physics. During his time at Oxford, he famously constructed a rudimentary computer out of a soldering iron, TTL gates, an M6800 processor, and a discarded television set. Following his graduation, he worked as an engineer at the telecommunications company Plessey in Poole, Dorset, and later at D.G. Nash, where he helped write typesetting software.

## The World Wide Web

The development of the World Wide Web was the result of Berners-Lee's efforts to solve the problem of information sharing among thousands of scientists working at CERN who utilized disparate hardware systems and network protocols.

### Early Prototypes at CERN

In 1980, Berners-Lee began working at CERN near Geneva, Switzerland, as an independent contractor. To help manage the complex web of researchers, projects, and systems, he proposed a project based on the concept of hypertext—text displayed on a computer screen that contains hyperlinks to other text. He built a prototype system called ENQUIRE (named after a Victorian advice manual, *Enquire Within Upon Everything*). Although ENQUIRE was never published or widely adopted, it formed the conceptual basis for his later work.

### The 1989 Proposal and Implementation

After a stint away from CERN, Berners-Lee returned as a fellow in 1984. By 1989, the internet (the physical network of connected computers) had already been established, but it lacked an intuitive, graphical interface for linking documents. In March 1989, Berners-Lee wrote a memo titled "Information Management: A Proposal," in which he envisioned a large hypertext database with typed links.

His manager, Mike Sendall, described the proposal as "vague but exciting" and allowed Berners-Lee to work on the system. Using a NeXT Computer, Berners-Lee developed three foundational technologies that remain the core of the Web:

* **HTML (Hypertext Markup Language):** The publishing format for the Web.
* **URI (Uniform Resource Identifier):** A kind of "address" that is unique and used to identify each resource on the Web (commonly known today as a URL).
* **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol):** The protocol allowing for the retrieval of linked resources from across the web.

By late 1990, he had written the first web browser (named WorldWideWeb) and the first web server software (CERN httpd). The first website, `info.cern.ch`, was published on August 6, 1991, providing an explanation of what the World Wide Web was and how one could use a browser and set up a web server.

### Public Domain Release

Perhaps Berners-Lee's most consequential decision was his successful petition to CERN management to release the Web's source code to the public domain. On April 30, 1993, CERN announced that the World Wide Web would be free to anyone, with no licensing fees attached. This open standard prevented the fragmentation of the system and catalyzed the explosive, decentralized growth of the modern internet.

## The World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)

In 1994, Berners-Lee left CERN to join the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), where he founded the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C). The W3C serves as the main international standards organization for the World Wide Web. By bringing together companies, academics, and researchers, the W3C develops interoperable technologies (specifications, guidelines, software, and tools) to ensure the Web's long-term growth and prevent monopolization by any single corporation. Berners-Lee has continuously served as the Director of the W3C since its inception.

## Later Initiatives and the Solid Project

Throughout the 21st century, Berners-Lee has focused on issues of data sovereignty, privacy, and net neutrality. In 2001, he co-authored a seminal paper on the "Semantic Web," a vision for an extension of the existing Web where information is given well-defined meaning, better enabling computers and people to work in cooperation. In 2009, he established the World Wide Web Foundation to advance the open Web as a public good and a basic right.

In 2016, Berners-Lee launched the Solid (Social Linked Data) project at MIT. Solid aims to fundamentally alter the current architectural model of the Web, which heavily centralizes user data in the hands of a few large technology platforms. The project provides a protocol for decentralized data storage, allowing users to control their own data in secure "Pods" and grant or revoke access to applications as they see fit. In 2018, he co-founded the startup Inrupt to accelerate the commercial adoption of the Solid ecosystem.

## Works or Publications

* "Information Management: A Proposal" (1989) — Internal CERN memo.
* *Weaving the Web: The Original Design and Ultimate Destiny of the World Wide Web by its Inventor* (1999) — Co-authored with Mark Fischetti.
* "The Semantic Web" (2001) — Co-authored with James Hendler and Ora Lassila, published in *Scientific American*.

